is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa,r1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: WAN edge routing policy between r1 (customer router) and ispa (upstream ISP). Specifically, ICMP Redirect handling on r1’s WAN interface and an incorrect default route on ispa.

Mechanism: ispa is sending ICMP Redirects back to r1 for internet-bound traffic, instructing r1 to use 203.0.113.2 (its own address) as the next hop. Because r1’s eth2 accepts redirects (net.ipv4.conf.eth2.accept_redirects = 1), Linux installs host routes in the route cache pointing to itself. That blackholes new external flows until the bad cache entry ages out (~5 seconds), producing the observed “hang then catch up” behavior and treacle-slow browsing. This manifests as 5-second stalls connecting to external IPs from clients (e.g., curl to http://1.1.1.1 totals ≈5.00s from both pc1 and laptop1), and on r1 we see ICMP Redirect Host messages from 203.0.113.1 with 100% ping loss to 1.1.1.1.

Localization: WAN link CD_ISP_R1 between r1.eth2 (203.0.113.2/30) and ispa.eth0 (203.0.113.1/30).

Root cause: ispa is misconfigured with a default route via the customer (default via 203.0.113.2), causing bogus ICMP redirects toward r1; r1 compounds this by accepting ICMP redirects on eth2.

Corrective fix:
- On ispa: remove the default route via 203.0.113.2 and point default to the true upstream (e.g., via 192.0.2.2 on eth1) or otherwise correct WAN routing.
- On r1: disable redirects on the WAN: sysctl net.ipv4.conf.eth2.accept_redirects=0 (and persist it), to prevent future cache poisoning by ICMP redirects. After changes, clear route cache or reload to restore fast, stable internet. 
=== END ===